# Escalate When Stuck — No Token-Burning Retries

When debugging failing tests (or any runtime failure), do **one** cheap pass. If that does not fix it, **stop and escalate to a human**. Do not keep retrying, re-running suites, or exploring alternate theories.

Debug runs: `yarn test -g "@tag" --retries=0 --reporter=line`. Never omit `--retries=0`.

## Allowed single pass (fix without escalating)

Only these count as a simple fix — do them once, then re-run the targeted test:

1. Read failure output + relevant POM / steps / feature
2. Obvious typo, wrong step wiring, or missing await
3. Locator already known or a clear one-line `data-testid` / role fix in the POM
4. Expected assertion string clearly wrong vs known UI text

## Escalate immediately (do not retry)

Stop after the first failed fix attempt (or sooner if the failure is clearly out of scope) when any of these apply:

- Failure persists after the simple pass above
- Root cause needs multi-step live-app flow discovery, data setup, env/auth, or app bugs
- You would need a second guess, another OpenCode browser pass, or another full suite run “to see if it helps”
- Same error class after one targeted re-run
- Flaky/timing/infra issues that are not a clear one-line wait fix

## How to escalate

Tell the human, briefly:

1. What failed (tag / scenario / error snippet)
2. What you already checked or changed
3. Why it is not a simple locator/wiring fix
4. What a human should look at next (hypothesis only — do not keep investigating)

## Hard limits

| Action | Limit |
|---|---|
| Fix attempts before escalate | **1** simple pass |
| Targeted re-runs after that pass | **1** |
| Full-suite re-runs while guessing | **0** |
| “Try another approach” loops | **0** — escalate instead |

This overrides any instinct to “keep going until green.” Token burn on repeated debug loops is worse than an early handoff.
